WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.560
Let's now look at the route function.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.600
The easiest way to create a route is directly from the navigation window using the

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.780
route button. Clicking it takes you to the route

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:25.880
editor. The map view remains the same, but the button bar on the right has changed.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.000
We'll now show you how to create a route, where to enter waypoints,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.520
and so on. Step one: Click

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:42.200
in the route widget in the lower left corner and create a new route by

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:49.080
giving it a new name by clicking "New". We'll choose the name "Test" and confirm with "OK".

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.880
The dialog box will now display the new name. A second click on "OK" will add

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.720
the route "Test" to the widget, but of course, there are no waypoints yet.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:07.920
Step two is setting waypoints. In AvNav, this is done by

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.760
placing the crosshairs on the point where you want to create a waypoint. Here, we'll choose the buoy west of

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:21.400
Drejö, or better yet, slightly to the side, for obvious reasons, and click the "After" button.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.600
Now we'll create a second waypoint. It should guide us into the passage

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.880
between Drejö and Avernakö. So, the crosshair needs to go there,

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.920
then we press "After" again. If we forgot

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:41.640
that we want to place a third waypoint between waypoints one and two,

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.400
we can now place a third waypoint between the two by clicking "Before.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:53.840
" This means waypoint number three will now be placed before the currently active waypoint two.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:02:00.120
It will look like this. That worked well. Working with the crosshair might

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.240
seem a bit cumbersome at first compared to direct taps on the

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:10.240
touchscreen. However, in situations with a rapidly moving ship on a smaller

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:17.360
screen and cold fingers, you'll come to appreciate this method of operation.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.640
Now it's time to learn about other routing functions, such as moving

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.720
an existing waypoint. For example, waypoint 1 should now

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.360
be moved to the approach to Aeroeskøbing. To do this, waypoint 1 must first

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:40.760
be selected. This is done by simply clicking directly on the waypoint in the map.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:47.520
If that's too cumbersome or too cramped, you can also select the waypoint in the widget.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:53.720
With waypoint 1 selected, the crosshair must be set to the new coordinates.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:03:01.280
Clicking the "Shift" button will then move waypoint 1 there.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:07.400
To delete a waypoint from the route, use the delete button. Of course,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:13.800
you must first select the waypoint you want to delete – as shown above.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:18.080
These are essentially the steps required to create a route.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:24.200
You can now access further options by clicking the Tools button and then

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:31.000
"show waypoint buttons." Here, you can use the Next and Previous buttons

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.640
to navigate between waypoints if working in the widget or on the map is less convenient.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.160
The Edit button allows you to edit each individual waypoint,

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:47.520
for example, to assign a new name. The Locate button lets you

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.680
quickly retrieve the selected waypoint and return it to the map window if the map

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:57.520
has been moved for any reason. You can also achieve this by

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:04.200
clicking the waypoint in the widget. There's always at least one other option, which is a good thing.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:11.160
Now that all the entries are complete, we move on to step 3: activating the route.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:17.240
This is done by clicking the Start button. Before doing so, you should double-check

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:25.120
which waypoint is currently active, as that's where the route will start. So, we'll set

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:32.040
waypoint 1 as the first starting point from our current position and click Start.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:38.040
The route is now active, and the view returns to the normal navigation window.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:43.280
The Start button changes to a Stop button. With this, you could deactivate the route

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:49.680
. You can also see that your route is now active because the widgets

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:56.360
display the route-related values: the distance to the next waypoint, the course to it, and,

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:03.840
in the RTE widget, the route length and ETA. Interesting functions can be accessed via the tools.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.480
The route can be activated while the route is running. If you decide

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.640
to skip a waypoint without changing the route's direction,

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:20.000
the Skip button is the right choice: Select the waypoint to be skipped,

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.960
then click Skip, and the route will jump to the next waypoint. This can be helpful

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.840
if you've passed a waypoint so far away that reaching it

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:37.480
isn't acknowledged. Of course, the route can also be modified while running

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:42.080
by switching back to the route editor,

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:49.480
for example, to move a waypoint. Waypoint 2, for instance, should

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:56.440
be moved closer to the buoy. This is done, as explained previously, using the Shift key.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:01.360
The change is applied immediately while the route is running. Saving the route

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:07.200
after it has been created or modified happens automatically in the background.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.400
If you want to perform an XTE update after modifying the route

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:16.880
, click Restart. To see

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:23.320
where the route files are located, navigate to the Routes section in the menu.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.840
All saved routes are listed there, and you can see that the "Test" route

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:34.000
was recently saved automatically. It can now be copied,

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:40.680
downloaded, or edited, and it can be placed as an overlay on a map.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.440
These are things you can try out at your leisure.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:51.760
What else is possible in the route section is pretty self-explanatory from the buttons.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:57.200
One button deserves special mention: "connect.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:03.320
" Normally, "connect" is active. This means that route operations are

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:08.680
executed on the server, and all connected displays show the same values.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.880
If "connect" is deactivated, you can

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:20.160
try out and change different routes on a local display—the server continues to work with the original route. These routes are

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.799
only processed on the local display. When

00:07:23.799 --> 00:07:23.972
working with a second display on a second device, the "connect" button should always be active
to ensure that the server and client (the second device) are connected. Otherwise,

00:07:23.972 --> 00:07:26.160
saving a route remains limited to the local machine.
You can find more detailed information in the documentation. That concludes our guide to creating routes.

